't really talked  about this yet but it's like I've been  experiencing like people was expecting  me to provide a lot for them um they you  know they read about what I got going on  and things like that and you know I  understand that I don't have those kind  of resources to help a lot of people so  we need more people that has you know a  lot of money and stuff like that  privilege and things like that to like  expand it and start putting together  systems that can really start helping  people with housing like when I own  first got out it was like hard to get  like holiday I kept getting turned down  so I'm working three jobs and things  like that like you can have the money to  put the down payment on places and  things and they won't accept you because  you formally incarcerated and there's  all kinds of like issues like that and  then jobs as well like we decided to be  artists which was like and that was one  of the things that connected us because  we had that in common that we wanted to  be artists you know no plan B plan a and  we was with and we we just got along we  was born together by Destiny with that  but what about all these other people  that don't have that kind of a drive or  whatever to think outside the box and  make another way when there's really no  way so I would just say like support  systems for um for many you know need  this needs to happen  yeah and I I mean completely agree with  what he's saying  um  I think that  I mean one of the things that I think is  really  I think important to kind of understand  is that  I I just did this conference in LA and  this this woman got up and was like  talking about  um dyslexia  and  uh kind of other forms of like um  um kind of like different ways of that  people learn and kind of access  information  and I forget the exact like statistic  but it's an incredibly High number of  people who are incarcerated who have  dyslexia or some other form of like  mental learning uh ability  and so  it's really interesting though because  like  for me anyway I I look at it and think  like okay  what I mean there's there's a number of  failings that are happening in the  system along the way right from housing  and job insecurity and racism and like  all the structural things that that we  kind of know exist  um uh within the education system  but it's also like how do we create  alternative systems and structures that  are kind of geared towards people who  you know think in a different way and  are kind of creative like they're not  going to be as successful sitting in a  classroom listening to a teacher talk  like maybe they need to be Hands-On and  they're more creative right  and so I I think it's like a very large  percentage of the prison population and  so part of my thinking is like how do we  actually create these other systems and  structures that allow people to learn in  a very very kind of individualized  tailored way  um and then how do we support them in  doing those things because I remember  going through the prison system and like  not one time did anyone ask me what I  was good at or what I was interested in  and it was always like you're bad at  this you did this you did that and it's  like always negative negative negative  nothing was to the positive  and so you know I think entire worlds  kind of start opening up when you ask  people like what are you good at what  are you interested in and then you  figure out how to kind of tailor and  support those things and that you know  that's like a very generalized way but I  do think once you start supporting  people like that then it has this ripple  effect where it's like I can't I can't  put his mask on until I put mine on  right and so like if you think of it  like that every person that you help  then has the capacity to help one or two  other people and it starts kind of  building this ripple effect  um but I think it starts by just you  know at least from our perspective like  trying to help an individual  um  versus like the big system changes where  we're also working and partnering with  Advocates who are trying to pass  policies and our artists try to change  the narrative so that people on the  ground who are voting on those policies  kind of understand what they're talking  about rather than like cold statistics  and numbers  um but it is it's like a very big  um complicated problem  we're seeing some of the change like in  terms of just perceptions when people  see the film right like we've been  screening it now at various festivals  and  um and we had our theatrical run and I  actually have I don't know about have  you guys experienced where you feel like  people come out of the theater asking  different questions than maybe they  might have if they hadn't seen the film  or feel or even I know like Gilberto  said that he feels like people maybe see  him differently like more are more open  or more positive about  interactions afterwards  um but hopefully we can kind of be a  part of that by continuing to to share  this and you're you know your courage  and sharing your stories has also I  think opened up we've had people in  audiences who share with me or with you  that they have been impacted by  incarceration directly but haven't felt  comfortable talking about it right  yeah the I think that is like an  important part it's like this film is  just as much about narrative change as  like the artists that we partner with  the works that we make it's it's all  kind of getting people to see something  from a much more human lens uh rather  than the sensationalized stories that  people were used to kind of digesting  about who we put in prisons  um and so yeah I've I've seen the I've  seen that I've had those experiences  after  um the screenings where people have come  up to me and talked to me I mean  numerous occasions  um and I think that is part of my  Collective healing right like a lot of  people feel such shame and stigma that  they don't even talk about it and so I  think like getting it to a place where  we can actually have conversations and  normalize talking about traumatic  experiences can actually help grow  something positive  so I want to finish with two questions  um where can we see the two of you where  where is there anywhere we can see your  work  um that you're doing currently  um is it social media or or are either  both of you having exhibits right now  for me uh one of the main places would  be Instagram  or like or because people be thinking  art or dot Craig dot T1 is when I post  things that's going on we have a few  things together and we have some things  coming up so definitely  um you know check out Instagram is where  I mostly post uh with squant um come  forth in the near future  yeah same for me Instagram is like a  great place to kind of like see what I'm  up to and knew where I post upcoming  exhibitions um I will say we both have  our in a show in Miami during Basel  um  what is that  December yeah early December right yeah  time is a flat circle at this point but  it's getting back to solitary time kind  of time  um but yes so we have a show coming up  in Miami uh at the end of this year and  um we will have a couple other shows  coming up early next year uh we just  I can't say anything at the moment and  you have um right of returnusa.com right  is the is the org website yeah um and  then we have crimesfilm.com for the for  the film that has links to all of their  websites that was my next question is  where can um other people who didn't get  to see this See Art and Crimes by crimes  totally yeah so um definitely follow  these guys on Instagram because they'll  they'll tell you you know where it's  playing and then also we have at crimes  film with a k  um and we have crimesfilm.com  um and the film's gonna be uh it's  playing in various museums and cultural  centers and theaters right now um for  the next couple of months and probably  Beyond and then also it's gonna be 